# OPEN limitation: constants are re-evaluated on every reference.
#
# `const k` compiles to a call of the zero-argument static method `c_k()`,
# whose body is the whole constant body, with no memoization. A DAG of N
# constants therefore costs 2^N on the JVM, while Peregrine's OCaml/C backends
# bind each constant once. The value is right, so `run` reports `ok`; the
# defect is the time, visible with `run --ref const-blowup`. Source:
# lambox-to-java-examples/src/ExampleConstBlowup.ard (N = blowupLevels = 30).
#
# Measured 2026-09-30 on the generated Prog.java, with `body()` pointed at
# a_N (java run only, -XX:-DontCompileHugeMethods; OCaml/C stay at ~0.0 s):
#
#   N           20     22     24     26     28     30
#   default   0.16   0.39   1.03   1.26   4.64  13.9 s
#   -Xint     0.86   2.00   7.15                     s   (~2x per level)
#
# HotSpot's inlining hides part of the growth (hence the uneven default row),
# which is why the program uses two interleaved chains: a single chain with two
# identical calls per body was collapsed completely above N = 26. The result
# is a nullary constructor, so the cost is time, not memory.
#
# The fix is a lazily initialized static field per constant; it needs static
# state in the Java model and changes the `const` case of the proof.
expected=1
backends=java ocaml c
runtime=peano
xfail=
note=Bool.true; a_k/b_k = pick of the previous pair: Java re-evaluates constants (2^N calls), OCaml/C bind them once
